 ## Overview
-Hermite interpolation for isosurface extraction determines the precise position of an isovertex along an edge where the scalar field value equals a specific threshold, utilizing both function values and gradients (Hermite data) to perform higher-order interpolation. It solves the problem of reconstruction artifacts caused by non-linear implicit functions, which linear interpolation (as used in [Linear Edge Intersection](868-linear-edge-intersection.md)) fails to capture accurately. By incorporating gradient information, it provides a more accurate isosurface representation for implicit functions that are not piecewise linear.
+Hermite interpolation for isosurface extraction determines the precise position of an isovertex along a zero-crossing edge by utilizing both scalar field values and gradient information (Hermite data) at the edge endpoints [S1]. Unlike [Linear Edge Intersection](868-linear-edge-intersection.md), which assumes linear variation and produces artifacts on non-linear implicit functions, this method fits higher-order polynomials to the local field behavior, significantly improving surface accuracy [S1]. It is primarily used in contouring algorithms like Marching Cubes where gradient data is available or can be approximated [S1].
 
